<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#gosanke">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gosanke</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gosanke, the 'three honourable houses', were the three senior branch families of the Tokugawa, founded by sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a>: the Owari, Kii and Mito houses. They ranked above all other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> and could supply an heir if the main line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shoguns</a> died out, as happened in 1716 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshimune">Tokugawa Yoshimune</a> of Kii became shogun.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gosanke.jpg" alt="Gosanke" width="198" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mon-Tokugawa.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After establishing his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> placed members of his family in key positions. His ninth son Yoshinao became daimyō of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagoya">Nagoya</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owari-province">Owari Province</a>, his tenth son Yorinobu daimyō of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wakayama">Wakayama</a> in Kii Province, and his eleventh son Yorifusa daimyō of Mito in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hitachi-province">Hitachi Province</a>; the houses they founded took their names from these <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a>. Ieyasu gave them the right to supply a shōgun so that the shogunate would not lack successors if the main line died out. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> the word could also mean other groupings of Tokugawa houses, such as the shogunal, Owari and Kii houses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Owari, the senior branch, held the Owari Domain from Nagoya Castle with a rating of 619,500 koku, the largest of the three, and was headed by 17 men before the han system ended; despite its seniority it never provided a shōgun. Yorinobu entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wakayama">Wakayama</a> in 1619, and the Kii house, rated at 555,000 koku, was the only one to supply shōguns directly, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshimune">Tokugawa Yoshimune</a> in 1716 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-iemochi">Tokugawa Iemochi</a> in 1858. Mito, rated at 250,000 koku and from 1710 at 350,000, was allowed to provide only the shōgun's vice; its heads included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-mitsukuni">Tokugawa Mitsukuni</a>.</p><p>Yoshimune, the fifth Tokugawa daimyō of Kii, founded three new houses on the model of the gosanke, the gosankyō, headed by two of his sons and a grandson, with lands that were scattered and smaller. Being closer to the shogunal house, the gosankyō later took over the gosanke's role of supplying a shōgun. One of them, the Hitotsubashi house, produced Tokugawa Ienari in 1787 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshinobu">Tokugawa Yoshinobu</a> in 1866; Yoshinobu, a son of the Mito house adopted into the Hitotsubashi in 1848, became the last shōgun.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> the heads of the three houses became marquesses under the kazoku system, and in 1929 the head of the Mito House was raised to duke. The houses outlived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> and the han system and continued into the 21st century, the Owari house linked to the Tokugawa Art Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagoya">Nagoya</a> and the Mito house to the Shōkōkan Tokugawa Museum in Mito, while the Kii House gave its historical properties to museums such as the Wakayama Prefectural Museum. In modern Japanese gosanke means the strongest or best-known three of anything, from the great hotels of Tokyo to the leading home computers of the 8-bit era.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gosanke” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kii became one of the three <b>Gosanke</b> houses entitled to supply a shogun.</blockquote><a href="/state/kii-domain">Kii Domain</a></li><li><blockquote>The domain, of about 350,000 koku, became one of the three <b>Gosanke</b> branch houses of the Tokugawa.</blockquote><a href="/state/mito-domain">Mito Domain</a></li><li><blockquote>With about 619,500 koku the Owari Tokugawa were the highest-ranking of the three <b>Gosanke</b> houses, yet no Owari lord ever became shogun.</blockquote><a href="/state/owari-domain">Owari Domain</a></li></ul></section>
